<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Taifa of Beja and Évora</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1144–1150</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Taifa of Beja and Evora</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">Taifa</a> of Beja and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evora">Évora</a> was a short-lived Muslim principality in the south of present-day Portugal, ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidray-ibn-wazir">Sidray ibn Wazir</a> in the 1140s when Almoravid rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> broke down. His family, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-wazir">Banu Wazir</a>, also took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badajoz">Badajoz</a> in 1145, and he moved between alliance and rivalry with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-qasi">Ibn Qasi</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mertola">Mértola</a>. The region came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> rule around 1150.</p></div>
